  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 9419513,
   "parent_id": 9419370,
   "author_user_id": 774651,
   "author_display_name": "Lol4t0",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/9419513",
   "score": 0,
   "created_utc": "2012-02-23T19:06:55Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2012-02-23T19:06:55Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<p>If you have something like this:</p>\n\n<pre><code>struct S\n{\n   int* p;\n   ~S() { delete p;}\n};\n\nS* s;\nstd::cout &lt;&lt; (void*)s;\ndelete s;\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>Probably, your program will crash while deleting <code>p</code>, but <code>s</code> will be printed. They will likely be different.</p>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 9419525,
   "parent_id": 9419370,
   "author_user_id": 5987,
   "author_display_name": "Mark Ransom",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/9419525",
   "score": 0,
   "created_utc": "2012-02-23T19:07:26Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2012-02-23T19:07:26Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<p>If you have a class with multiple inheritance, the address will be adjusted depending on the type of the pointer. <code>free</code> will need to use the base type, but the compiler should be able to do the proper conversion invisibly behind the scene.</p>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 9419537,
   "parent_id": 9419370,
   "author_user_id": 198536,
   "author_display_name": "wallyk",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": true,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/9419537",
   "score": 0,
   "created_utc": "2012-02-23T19:08:02Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2012-02-23T19:08:02Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<p>Almost certainly, the pointer was <code>delete</code>d before the delete you are looking at.  Deleting a pointer twice causes exactly the symptoms described.</p>\n"
  },
